From jeff@artud2.ENET.DEC.COM Mon Apr 16 14:22:22 1990

Did you restart the inetd? Find it's pid and do a kill -1 <pid> for it
to reread the /etc/inetd.conf. I am including some quick notes on it.
One thing to remember though, is that if you modify the tftp line in
/etc/inetd.conf to be tftpd, without the -r /<dir> option you are open
to a known security hole. My recommendation is to created the
/tftpboot/vt1000 directory and use symbolic links to get to the fonts
in /usr/lib/X11/fonts/decwin/75dpi and 100dpi. This way your
inetd.conf file can be:

 tftp	dgram	udp	nowait	/usr/etc/tftpd -r /tftpboot

Hope this helps.

Fonts
=====

The documentation, to put it mildly, contains a few errors. First, you must
use UWS 2.2 RISC fonts for the VT1000. UWS 2.1 RISC Fonts will not work.
UWS 2.1 VAX fonts will work if you wish to test clients applications from a
VAX ULTRIX node if you don't have UWS2.2. You only need the fonts, not the
entire UWS 2.2 release running on the client. I did it this way with my
system here. If you do not have UWS 2.2 anywhere, I can make you a font
tape. However,
I will be out of the office until Wednesday of next week.


To start the process:

This font business is documented in "Installing and Using The VT1000
Video Terminal" in Appendix E, "System Manager Tasks", pages 244-248.
On page 246, they have a sample inetd.conf file. This is what mine looks like:

#
# @(#)inetd.conf	4.1.1.4	(ULTRIX)	4/19/88
# Internet server configuration database
#
#echo	stream	tcp	nowait	/etc/miscd	echod
#echo	dgram	udp	wait	/etc/miscd	echod
#discard	stream	tcp	nowait	/etc/miscd	sinkd
#discard	dgram	udp	wait	/etc/miscd	sinkd
systat	stream	tcp	nowait	/etc/miscd	systatd
#systat	dgram	udp	wait	/etc/miscd	systatd
daytime	stream	tcp	nowait	/etc/miscd	daytimed
#daytime	dgram	udp	wait	/etc/miscd	daytimed
quote	stream	tcp	nowait	/etc/miscd	quoted
#quote	dgram	udp	wait	/etc/miscd	quoted
#chargen	stream	tcp	nowait	/etc/miscd	chargend
#chargen	dgram	udp	wait	/etc/miscd	chargend
#ftp	stream	tcp	nowait	/usr/etc/ftpd	ftpd
finger	stream	tcp	nowait	/usr/etc/fingerd	fingerd
#telnet	stream	tcp	nowait	/etc/telnetd	telnetd
ftp	stream	tcp	nowait	/usr/etc/ftpd.gw	ftpd
telnet	stream	tcp	nowait	/usr/etc/telnetd.gw	telnetd
#time	stream	tcp	nowait	/etc/miscd	timed
time	dgram	udp	wait	/etc/miscd	timed
#tftp	dgram	udp	nowait	/usr/etc/tftpd	tftpd -r /tmp
tftp	dgram	udp	nowait	/usr/etc/tftpd	tftpd 
exec	stream	tcp	nowait	/etc/rexecd	rexecd
login	stream	tcp	nowait	/etc/rlogind	rlogind
shell	stream	tcp	nowait	/etc/rshd	rshd
comsat	dgram	udp	wait	/etc/comsat	comsat
talk	dgram	udp	wait	/etc/talkd	talkd
ntalk	dgram	udp	wait	/usr/etc/ntalkd		ntalkd
bootp   dgram   udp     wait    /usr/etc/bootpd     bootpd -i

Note: I could not get this to properly load fonts using tftp -r fontdir.
Basically, I put entire font pathnames in the font.paths file.

On the VT1000, you would put the internet id of the font host plus the path
of the location of the font file (containing listing of all fonts)

i.e. TFTP Font Path | ROM,128.45.52.250:/tftpboot/vt1000/font.paths


The font.paths file has complete pathnames to font files. Only files
that are fonts should be in a font directory or the loading will fail.

To load fonts from VT1000, click on "Apply" and on "Acknowledge" box (just for
good luck with "watch icon" - we're not sure this does anything or not). This
will take 5-7 minutes or so. Click on "Acknowledge" after TFTP Font Path Load
is finished (message will change in message window). While fonts are loading,
the tftpd should be starting and stopping with a new pid each time. If it is 
not doing this, fonts are not loading.

Just ignore our sed script examples in the documentation and use whatever tool
you prefer to get entire pathnames in the font.paths file.

I hope this is helpful.

font.paths (should be world readable):

/tftpboot/vt1000/75dpi/avantgarde_book10.pcf
       ... billions and billions of file names deleted... :-)
/tftpboot/vt1000/75dpi/variable.pcf
