From sandstro@cs.washington.edu Mon Apr 16 18:32:21 1990

I set up a vt1000 here at the University of Washington Department
of Computer Science and Engineering 10 days ago.  The vt1000 hardware
is SN TA 00603547, the documentation is ``Draft Edition 12/27/89''.
In addition to the 36 fonts in ROM, I set up 288 fonts via tftp.
The fonts are on a VAX 3500 in a place where a chrooted tftpd can read.
Here is how I did it, in outline:

I found a directory tree with X11 R4 (the free X software).
I made a directory tree with real directories, but every file a symbolic
link pointing the the X11 R4 software. (with lndir.sh)
Then, in the tree that is 99% links,

I replaced ./mit/fonts with ./contrib/server/pcf/fonts.
I replaced config/ultrix.cf with one that changes FontDefines to be
#define FontDefines -DFONT_BDF -DFONT_PCF -DCOMPRESSED_FONTS
I built the fonts directory.

Then, on a different machine.
I put bdftopcf, dxmkfontdir, and showpcf in my path.
I laid out the .bdf files I wanted to use on the vt1000.
I built .pcf files.
Then, following page 247 of the vt1000 manual (end of Appendix
on Networks and X Windows), I constructed a file ``font.paths''
that contains the full pathnames of the pcf files in the chrooted
file system.  I set the font path to
ROM,128.x.x.x:/vt1000/font.paths
Saved the font info into non-volatile memory, and rebooted.

The tftp font reading is really slow.  One successful boot
took 15 minutes.  Perhaps when the vt1000 becomes a product,
this will be faster.

