Until dec started renting software fairly recently you could only get
paid-up license good for a given version (and precedecessors) of the
software.  the maintenance feed for self-maintenance gave you the
right to install new version of the software on the system (updating the 
maximum version your paid up license was good for as new releases came out
and depending on the period (i.e. which of several packages were called 
self-maintenance) might include media kits as well.

So given what you said in your message it sounds like you can freeze your
system as is for eternity.  Just don't expect DEc help if something breaks.
Even hardware maintenance becomes problematical after a while as it 
becomes harder to test systems for which the new diagnostics are not tested
fully.  But that is a slower process and just degrades speed (total box swaps
work fairly well for a long time and eventually thats what you degenerate into.
Of course with that level of support you can go with 3rd party maitnenance
and save the the "digital Difference".($))


