In article <5236@helios.ee.lbl.gov>, envbvs@epb2.lbl.gov (Brian V.
Smith) writes:
> 
> Allright, DEC, let's here the explanation for this.
> A few months ago, there was a big discussion about why the DECstations
> didn't use any graphics accellerators because of the speed of the CPU,
> memory, etc. traded off against the cost of developing a specialized 
> graphics chip.
> 
> What's the deal?  1/2 :-)

Well, now that I can legally reply...

The argument w.r.t. the DECstation 3100 was never that accelerators
weren't worthwhile, but that they weren't on that product, for specific
reasons.  The only accelerators we had available within DEC "off the
shelf" during the design period for the DS3100 were those used in the
GPX-type workstations (VAXstation II/GPX, VAXstation 2000 and VAXstation
3100) or the firefox workstation (VAXstation 3520/3540).  

In the first case, the DS3100 color frame buffer had about the same
performance as the GPX, so adding it into the design would increase cost
and complexity while providing little increase performanc.e

In the second case, the firefox graphics subsystem was higher in
performance, but too complex to use in a low-end design.  A design that
would have allowed it as an option would have been substantially more
expensive even without the option.

Since no existing subsystem were ready to go, we decided to go with the
"RISC" approach to system design, and make the fast CPU earn it's living.  

We've been working on the accelerator hardware here in Palo Alto since
before the DECstation 3100 was shipped;  it provides a very nice balance
for the high compute capacity of the machine.

I'd say more, but it would sound like an advertisement.

