Actually, Richard's comments don't go far enough...

When we started working on PMAX (DECstation 3100), an off the cuff 
estimate Joel McCormack and I did indicated we'd do at least as well 
as the GPX hardware with just a frame buffer.

History showed we (or I should say, Joel McCormack and Todd Newman) 
did much better, for almost everything, than the GPX hardware 
(3-4 times the text painting speed, 3-4 times
the vectors/second, tile and stipple fill faster, only scroll
ended up slower).  So there just wasn't any hardware worth
using available at the time.  

So at the time the decision was made, we thought that hardware 
would have just added cost, but not helped performance.  A good 
reason not to throw hardware at a problem, so we didn't.

In retrospect, we lucked out, it turns out that hardware on the machine would
have slowed the machine down greatly and made it more expensive; a bad 
combination.

So here we are 14 months later; the silicon is faster overall (and our
X implementation on the silicon actually gets the speed), but more expensive
than a frame buffer; at least one might think 
this is a resonable combination. (Even now, the frame buffer
does some operations better than the "accelerator" can, though
most applications will do much better with the hardware).
It wouldn't have been on the 3100, given what we could
have built then.

All of these situations go back and forth, depending upon lots
of factors; CPU speed, frame buffer bandwidth and latency, tricks
you might pull in silicon, brain-death in the silicon, etc.  
But people often get it wrong, and pay good money for hardware
that slows a machine down.

Don't believe anything except how your applications run....
Theoretical hardware numbers are completely useless as a predictor
of system speed.  Slightly less useless are numbers through the
entire software system (like x11perf), which if I understand an
application very well I might be able to make some approximate predictions
from.  But ultimately, only your applications can tell for sure.
Graphics/window system benchmarking makes CPU benchmarking look
trivial.  You've all seen the marketing snake oil put out all too
often for CPU benchmarking... There are many more free variables in
graphics than for scalar CPU performance.
			- Jim


