In article <54424@bbn.COM> koolish@bbn.com (Dick Koolish) writes:
>Does X have any notion of scrolling?  I would like to create
>a large image in memory but view a smaller part of it on the screen.
>To scroll the window over the image, the only ways I have found so far
>are to use either XCopyArea to put up a different part of a pixmap,
>or XPutImage to display a different part of an XImage.  Both seem to
>be complete redisplay operations rather than scrolling operations.
>Is there any more appropriate way to do this?


Imagine that you create a window, win1, and a window within this window, win2.
Usually win2 will be smaller than, and will reside fully inside, it's parent
window as shown below.


			------------------------
			|win1                  |
			|                      |
			|   --------           |
			|   |win2  |           |
			|   |      |           |
			|   |      |           |
			|   |      |           |
			|   --------           |
			|                      |
			|                      |
			------------------------


Now imagine that win2 was moved---the XMoveWindow() function can be used to
do this---down a bit. The result would be something like:

			------------------------
			|win1                  |
			|                      |
			|                      |
			|                      |
			|                      |
			|                      |
			|                      |
			|   --------           |
			|   |win2  |           |
			|   |      |           |
			------------------------
			    :      :            
			    ........            

where the dotted lines indicate that part of win2 lies outside win1. Since
a window is always "clipped" by it's parent window, only the part of win2 which
lies within win1 will de displayed (so the rectangular section marked with dots
would not be visible on the screen).

Now imagine that win2 happens to be bigger than win1 like shown below:


	.....................................................
	:win2                                               :
	:			------------------------    :
	:			|win1                  |    :
	:			|                      |    :
	:			|                      |    :
	:			|                      |    :
	:			|                      |    :
	:			|                      |    :
	:			|                      |    :
	:			|                      |    :
	:			|                      |    :
	:			|                      |    :
	:			------------------------    :
	:                                                   :
	:                                                   :
	:                                                   :
	.....................................................

In this case win1 will show a small part of win2. The part of win2 which lies
outside win1 will not be displayed. Or to put it differently, only the part of
win2 which lies inside win1 will be visible (i.e. win2 is still "clipped" by
it's parent window). If win2 was moved then win1 will display a different part
of win2. This can be seen by the following diagrams which show some text in
win2.


	.....................................................
	:win2                                               :
	:			------------------------    :
	:			|win1                  |    :
	:			|                      |    :
	: Once upon a time there|lived a princess who w|s   :
	: very pretty. However, |he was also very lonel|.   :
	: A handsome knight in s|ining armour married h|r   :
	: and they all lived hap|ily every after.      |    :
	:			|                      |    :
	:			|                      |    :
	:			|                      |    :
	:			|                      |    :
	:			------------------------    :
	:                                                   :
	:                                                   :
	:                                                   :
	.....................................................

Move win2 right 3 character spaces using XMoveWindow() to get

	   .....................................................
	   :win2                                               :
	   :		        ------------------------       :
	   :		        |win1                  |       :
	   :		        |                      |       :
	   : Once upon a time th|re lived a princess wh| was   :
	   : very pretty. Howeve|, she was also very lo|ely.   :
	   : A handsome knight i| shining armour marrie| her   :
	   : and they all lived |appily every after.   |       :
	   :		        |                      |       :
	   :		        |                      |       :
	   :		        |                      |       :
	   :		        |                      |       :
	   :		        ------------------------       :
	   :                                                   :
	   :                                                   :
	   :                                                   :
	   .....................................................

Notice that win1 stayed in the same place on the screen. The effect is that
win2 has scrolled left. So to scroll win2 left we actually move it right! 
Similarly to scroll up we move win2 down.

When you do the XMoveWindow(), X will, with a bit of luck, have the sense to
scroll what it can and it will only generate an expose event for the section
of the window that has become visible as a result of the XMoveWindow(). Thus,
for the above case, the rectanged specified in the expose event would be
as indicated below marked as "###"

	   .....................................................
	   :win2                                               :
	   :		        ------------------------       :
	   :		        |###1                  |       :
	   :		        |###                   |       :
	   : Once upon a time th|###lived a princess wh| was   :
	   : very pretty. Howeve|###he was also very lo|ely.   :
	   : A handsome knight i|###ining armour marrie| her   :
	   : and they all lived |###ily every after.   |       :
	   :		        |###                   |       :
	   :		        |###                   |       :
	   :		        |###                   |       :
	   :		        |###                   |       :
	   :		        ------------------------       :
	   :                                                   :
	   :                                                   :
	   :                                                   :
	   .....................................................

Your application would then have to repaint the exposed rectangle. If you're
using a pixmap to retain the picture then you can simply use XCopyArea() to do
the necessary.

That's the theory. If I got any of it wrong then I'm sure somebody on the net
will correct me.

Since this is something that is commonly needed, it has all been encapsulated
in various widget sets. In the Athena widget set, for example, win1 would be
a Viewport widget. The viewport would optionally have horizontal and/or
vertical scrollbars. When the user scrolls using a scrollbar, the viewport
will automatically execute the code necessary to move the inner widget. I've
used it once in an application and was very plesantly surprised just how easy
it all was. I'm sure that other toolkits provide something similar to Athena's
Viewport.

Hope that's of some help,
Ciaran.
