4-3 DECSYSTEM 5800 FACT SHEET

Product Name:  DECsystem 5800 Series
               - New DECsystem 5830 and DECsystem 5840 Computers
               - Price Reduction on DECsystem 5810 and DECsystem
	       	 5820

Overview:

The DECsystem 5800 series includes the most powerful, most
expandable multiuser members of Digital's family of compatible
UNIX(R)-based RISC systems.  DECsystem 5800 computers balance
high CPU speed, large storage capacity and system memory, and
high I/O throughput rates to handle the demanding processor and
data requirements of large work groups.  Running
ULTRIX--Digital's  implementation of the UNIX operating
system--the DECsystem 5800 series uses symmetric multiprocessing
(SMP) to make efficient use of its multiple processors.  The
series delivers performance of from 18.7 up to 62 Integer MIPS*,
and SPECthruput** ratings of 1@10.0 to 4@9.3.

Since its announcement in July of 1989, the DECsystem 5800 series
has gained market acceptance with large-system users in  both
commercial and technical applications.  At announcement, the
series included the uniprocessor DECsystem 5810 and the
dual-processor DECsystem 5820.  The series is now being extended
to include the three-processor DECsystem 5830 and the
four-processor DECsystem 5840.  The new DECsystem 5840 provides
more than three times the processing power of the DECsystem 5810
in the same footprint.

In addition, prices on the DECsystem 5810 and DECsystem 5820 are
being reduced by 40%.  With an entry-system price of $75,000, the
DECsystem 5800 series brings Digital's RISC performance to new
groups of users.

The other members of Digital's binary-compatible family of RISC
systems include the DECsystem 3100, DECsystem 5000 Model 200, and
DECsystem 5400 computers; and the DECstation 2100, DECstation
3100, and the DECstation 5000 Model 200 workstations.

Key Features:
    o The 40% price reduction on DECsystem 5810 and DECsystem
      5820 computers underscores Digital's commitment to
      delivering excellent price/performance for the DECsystem
      family.
    o The DECsystem 5800's modular system expansion enables
      customers to buy the system they need now and expand as
      their needs grow:
       - up to four CPUs
       - up to 256 Mbytes of standard ECC memory
       - up to 115 Gbytes disk storage
       - up to five VAXBI channels for more I/O capacity
       - up to four Ethernet connections
    o  The new DECsystem 5830 and DECsystem 5840 computers extend
       the growth path of the binary-compatible DECsystem family
       up to 62 Integer MIPS.
    o  Upgrading to higher levels of performance within the
       series takes only minutes and is a simple matter of adding
       boards to the existing cabinet.
    o  ULTRIX with SMP makes efficient use of each processor,
       delivering performance from 18.7 up to 62 Integer MIPS and
       SPECthruput ratings of 1@10.0 to 4@9.3, with aggregate
       throughput of 10.0 to 37.2 times a VAX-11/780 system.
    o  All systems are supported by ULTRIX V4.0, which includes
       an optimized C compiler and a wide range of UNIX utilities
       and tools.  Among the available optional compilers is a
       new version of the optimizing FORTRAN compiler, and new
       optimizing Pascal and Lucid Common Lisp compilers.
    o  All systems now include in-cabinet storage.

System Description:

The DECsystem 5800 series is designed to allow maximum system
configuration flexibility.  The system is housed in the 60-inch
tall cabinet which accommodates the CPUs, memory, I/O adapters,
in-cabinet storage, plus the TK70 load device.   DECsystem 5800
computers can support up to  256 Mbytes of memory, up to 115
Gbytes of storage, one to five VAXBI I/O channels for throughput
of up to 50 Mbytes/sec, and one to four Ethernet  connections for
support of a large number of workstations/users.  The CPU  module
uses the MIPS Computer Systems, Inc. R3000 processor (running at
25 Mhz) and the R3010 floating point unit, and is incorporated
into Digital's XMI-based system platform.  The system also
features two levels of cached memory.

