In article <1990Apr5.123703.2251@maths.tcd.ie> ajudge@maths.tcd.ie (Alan Judge) writes:
>Has anybody come up with a satisfactory method of preventing users from
>resetting DECStations and booting into single user mode?

>I am thinking of implementing this by running a program from /.profile which
>detects single user mode (somehow? getpid() == 3?), disables interrupts,
>and demands the root password.

I made the same on workstations, I don't think it differ on
decstations.

ASSUME that root uses csh (not sh!!!) when login, you would not have
to check for single user. /.profile will be executed only in single
user mode.

/bin/login is a symlink to /usr/bin/login, in single user /usr is not
always available and if not you will find yourself in trouble.  So rm
the link and make a real copy of login in /bin. Csh must get the same
treatment,

Another point to remember is that on Ultrix workstations /etc/ttys is
usually configured to eliminate login on console (and enable ::0 - X
vitual terminal - instead). Keep it off but mark it as secure (if you
did not make it already).

Now include these lines in /.profile

echo -n "Single user "
login root

Test it before you trust it.
