Instead of what all the other answers told, the problem is not the
remote shell but the restore program itself. It simply means :
   restore must be used interactively.

I am using a special system which does make dumps with use of the
crontab file. To restore these dumps I discovered this "feature" of
restore. Let me give you an example :

There will be 2 systems, the source and the target.
A dump has been made from a filesystem of the source to a file on the
target. Using the same type of commands as the crontab discussed in the
previous article you could restore the partition using one of the
following methods :

1) from the target :   cat dumpfile | rsh source restore -rf -
2) from the source :   rsh source cat dumpfile | restore -rf -

(I left out the home directory for the restore to simplify the example)
Normally both commands would work but because restore needs a tty to
write its diagnostics to, only option 2 works.

I hope this answers your question.

